COMMAND NAME: groupSession

Starts an interactive command-line session with multiple hosts specified
in a host file. Will also perform SSH key exchanges as the current user 
(or the user specified in the host file).


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

groupSession <host_file> [-p ssh_port] [-s] [-v] [-?]


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The groupSession utility starts an interactive command-line session that 
allows you to run bash shell commands on multiple hosts at the same time. 
The groupSession utility uses a host list file to determine the hosts 
(and optionally the users) involved in a session. You cannot change the 
host list file mid-session. If you want to change the host list file, 
you must start a new groupSession instance after editing the file. 

The groupSession utility will also exchange SSH keys between the hosts 
at the start of a session if you do not have a trusted host environment 
established already. This allows SSH connections to the remote hosts 
without a password prompt. If a user name is not specified in the host 
list file, then the currently logged in user will be used. 

Greenplum recommends performing the key exchange process twice: once 
as root and once as the bgadmin user (the user designated to own your 
Greenplum database installation). The Greenplum database management scripts require 
that the same non-root user be created on all hosts in the Greenplum database 
system, and the scripts must be able to connect as that user to all hosts 
without a password prompt. When exchanging SSH keys, hit the ENTER key 
when prompted for the ssh-agent pass key phrase (do not enter a pass 
key phrase). For example:

groupSession: Remote password for host kite6: <user_password>
groupSession: ssh-agent pass key phrase: [ENTER]

Once your trusted host environment is established, you get the groupSession 
command prompt (>). You can enter a command as you would in a regular bash 
terminal command-line, and the command will be executed on all hosts in the 
host list file. To run a command on the local host only, prefix the command 
with ! or shell. Use rcopy to copy a file to a specified location on all 
hosts.

ABOUT PATHS AND USERS

If a user name is not specified in the host list file, groupSession will 
execute commands as the currently logged in user. To determine the 
currently logged in user, do a whoami command. If changing users, you should 
force a login by using a dash character (-) after the su command. 
For example:

su - [user_name]

By default, groupSession goes to $HOME of the current user on the remote 
hosts after login. To ensure commands are executed correctly on all 
remote hosts, you should always enter absolute paths.

*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

<host_file>

Required. The name of a file that contains a list of hosts that will 
participate in the group session. The groupSession utility will also 
exchange SSH keys as either the current user or the username specified 
in the host file. The format of the host file is as follows:

username@host1
username@host2
...
username@hostN

OR (if you want to connect as the current user)

host1
host2
...
hostN



-p <ssh_port>

Optional. If you are using a different SSH port besides the default 
port of 22, use this option to specify the SSH port number.


-s

Optional. Disable the setup of trusted host key exchange. The default 
behavior is to exchange SSH keys as the specified user between the 
current host and the hosts in the host list.

-v

Optional. Verbose mode will report all commands sent to a host and 
the output received.

-h

Displays the online help.


*****************************************************
COMMANDS AT THE GROUPSESSION PROMPT
*****************************************************

At the groupSession command prompt (>), you can enter a command as you 
would in a regular bash terminal command-line and the command will be 
executed on all hosts in the host list file.

To run a command on the local host only, prefix the command with ! or shell. 

Use the rcopy command to copy a file to a specified location on all hosts.

*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Start groupSession and exchange SSH keys as the current user with the 
hosts listed in host_file:

./groupSession host_file -v

groupSession: Remote password for host kite6: <user_password>
groupSession: ssh-agent pass key phrase: [ENTER]
groupSession: Remote password for host kite7: <user_password>
groupSession: ssh-agent pass key phrase: [ENTER]
Keys for mppadmin on kite 7 have been installed.
Keys for mppadmin on kite 6 have been installed.
Acquired passwords, keys.
Entering command prompt, ssh commands are executed on all hosts:
>


At the groupSession command prompt, copy a file to the specified 
location on all the hosts in host_file.

rcopy mpp-2.0.tar.gz /

At the groupSession command prompt, run a shell command on 
all the hosts in host_file.

ls -a /mppdata/dbfast1

At the groupSession command prompt, run a shell command on the 
local host only.

shell cat /etc/profile
OR
! cat /etc/profile
